findings.description:
100-arch says the engine "is stateless between ticks — all state lives in the arrays passed to it, including the spatial index," and that the world server owns the spatial index (100-arch, Envisioned State). The 140 doc adds three things that break this without saying so:
- cached neighbor lists across ticks (119, line 86);
- an engine-owned GPU spatial hash, rebuilt in-tick (line 84);
- GPU-resident fields from Tier 2 on.

Each moves mutable state across the engine boundary, and no owner is named. Amend 100-arch or name the owner of each state object. 100-arch also asks the engine to define its internal precision (100-arch line 98), which this doc does not do. 300-arch says nothing flows back from clients into simulation state in v1 (line 78). The camera channel (line 90) is client-to-server. It is view-only and therefore compatible, but the doc should say so.

Culling (line 90) and delta encoding (line 88) are per-client. At 20 clients (the 300-arch stretch target, line 56) and 2M agents:
- about 40M visibility tests per tick, or about 2.4×10⁹ per second at 60 Hz;
- per-client last-sent state of about 12 MB (int16) to 24 MB (float32) per client, or 240–480 MB in total;
- per-client quantization and framing of up to 6M values per streamed frame, in NumPy, at several milliseconds per client (my estimate).

The doc treats culling as "layered on top of whatever the engine produces" (line 76). That understates it. At Tier 4 the contract decides whether positions leave the GPU at all (line 72), so these components are not layered. The wire track bundles four mechanisms with different state owners: serialization (stateless), decimation (server clock), delta (per-client sent state), and culling (per-client view state). Split them and name each owner.
